    question: 'Should I use a disc stack centrifuge or a decanter on food processing wastewater?',
    answer:
      'It depends on how much solid is in the stream. A decanter handles high-sludge wastewater and produces a dewatered cake continuously. A disc stack centrifuge polishes lower-solids water and removes fine suspended particles and oil. Many food plants use both: the decanter first, then a disc stack machine as a polisher. See <a href="/difference-between-decanter-centrifuge-disc-centrifuge/">decanter vs disc stack centrifuge</a>.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can a centrifuge remove fats, oils and grease from wastewater?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Industrial wastewater often carries oil as well as sludge, and a three-phase centrifuge separates the oil, the water, and the solids in one machine. Dolphin has built grease trap waste and yellow grease systems on a three-phase Alfa Laval disc stack machine with a decanter upstream for bulk solids. See <a href="/yellow-grease-purification-centrifuge/">yellow grease purification</a>.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Will a centrifuge reduce BOD?',
